WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.560
A key rule for all marine life is to avoid touching it, even if it looks dead, as it

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.480
can still sting.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:12.500
Marine life stings can range from a mild nuisance to a life-threatening emergency, so your first

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:17.080
priority is always scene safety and removing the active threat, like getting away from

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.680
a live jellyfish.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:23.559
If someone is stung, you should look for signs like pain, redness, or bleeding, and immediately

00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:27.059
get them to a safe place to rinse the area with seawater.

00:00:27.059 --> 00:00:32.380
If tentacles are still attached, use a towel, a stick, or a gloved hand to remove them carefully.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.259
Never use your bare hands.

00:00:34.259 --> 00:00:38.939
It is vital that you do not rub the area, as rubbing can actually trigger the tentacles

00:00:38.939 --> 00:00:41.299
to release more venom into the skin.

00:00:41.299 --> 00:00:45.240
Once the material is removed, the best way to treat the pain is to soak the affected

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:51.580
area in tolerably hot water, ideally between 104 and 113 degrees Fahrenheit, for about

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.540
20 to 45 minutes.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.740
If a soak isn't possible, a hot shower can also work.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:00.700
While you might have heard about using vinegar, its effectiveness depends on the specific

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:07.019
species, so if you aren't sure, stick to the hot water soak and call poison control for guidance.

00:01:07.019 --> 00:01:10.500
Finally, keep a very close watch on the person's breathing.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.820
You should call 911 immediately if they develop any breathing problems, experience swelling

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.419
in the chest or throat, faint, or show any signs of shock.

